be a very close sub- stitute for web search. Writing includes the automated production of emails, documents and other communications, but also editing, critiquing, summarizing, and translating text provided by the user. Writing is the most common use case at work, accounting for 40% of work-related messages on average in June 2025. About two-thirds of all Writing messages ask ChatGPT to modify user text (editing, critiquing, translating, etc.) rather than creating new text from scratch. About 10% of all messages are requests for tutoring or teaching, suggesting that education is a key use case for ChatGPT. Two of our findings stand in contrast to other work. First, we find the share of messages related to computer coding is relatively small: only 4.2% of ChatGPT messages are related to computer programming, compared to 33% of work-related Claude conversations Handa et al. (2025).8 Second, we find the share of messages related to companionship or social-emotional issues is fairly small: only 1.9% of ChatGPT messages are on the topic of Relationships and Personal Reflection and 0.4% are related 7The difference between Practical Guidance and Seeking Information is that the former is highly customized to the user and can be adapted based on conversation and follow-up, whereas the latter is factual information that should be the same for all users. For example, users interested in running might ask ChatGPT for the Boston Marathon qualifying times by age and gender (Seeking Information), or they might ask for a customized workout plan that matches their goals and current level of fitness (Practical Guidance). 8Handa et al. (2025) report that 37% of conversations are mapped to a “computer and mathematical” occupation category, and their Figure 12 shows 30% or more of all imputed tasks are programming or IT-related. We believe the discrepancy is partly due to the difference in types of users between Claude and ChatGPT, additionally Handa et al. (2025) only includes queries that ”possibly involve an occupational task”. 2"], "ls_example_dataset_split": ["base"], "ls_run_depth": 0}, "runtime": {"sdk": "langsmith-py", "sdk_version": "0.4.5", "library": "langsmith", "platform": "macOS-15.6.1-arm64-arm-64bit-Mach-O", "runtime": "python", "py_implementation": "CPython", "runtime_version": "3.13.6", "langchain_version": "0.3.26", "langchain_core_version": "0.3.68"}}, "run_type": "chain", "id": "c500126f-17f0-4fa6-bb65-9674be186b6e", "status": "success"}

roasted-event-66_outputs:
{"output": "Yo, buckle up—18 billion messages a week blasting through ChatGPT by July 2025 isn't just a number; it’s a seismic ripple aka a digital tsunami shaking the foundations of how society vibes with AI. We’re talking about nearly 10% of the global adult population plugged into this neural net, firing off queries that go way beyond just work stuff—over 70% of those messages groove outside the 9-to-5 grind. That signals a wild paradigm shift where AI is seamlessly integrating into our everyday lives, turbocharging not only productivity but personal creativity, learning, and problem-solving.

This explosion in non-work AI usage is straight-up redefining human welfare on a massive scale, pumping up quality of life and knowledge accessibility worldwide. Plus, the fact that the usage surge is global and especially fierce in low- and middle-income countries means AI isn’t just a Silicon Valley flex—it’s leveling the playing field, fueling empowerment, and sparking growth in places previously starved for tech revolution.

So, the 18 billion weekly missives? They’re a blazing spotlight showing that AI adoption isn’t some niche futurist fantasy—it’s a booming, everyday reality rewriting society’s playbook with radical speed and inclusivity. That’s dope evolution in action."}

